  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Suppose $f:X\\to Y$ and $g:Y\\to X$ are functions such that $g(f(x))=x$ for all $x\\in X$. Prove that $f$ is injective and $g$ is surjective.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f$ is injective and $g$ is surjective.\n\nApplying $g$ to equal outputs of $f$ gives equal inputs. Every $x\\in X$ has the preimage $f(x)\\in Y$ under $g$.",
      "worked": "Let $a,b\\in X$ and suppose $f(a)=f(b)$. Applying $g$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}g(f(a))&=g(f(b))\\\\a&=b.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $f$ is injective.\n\nLet $x\\in X$. Choose $y=f(x)\\in Y$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}g(y)&=g(f(x))\\\\g(y)&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nEvery element of $X$ is the image of an element of $Y$, so $g$ is surjective.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
